Metadata-Version: 2.4
Name: data-product-actions
Version: 0.1.0
Summary: Workflow engine actions that create a data product and set its lifecycle stage
Requires-Python: >=3.10
Description-Content-Type: text/markdown

# data-product-actions

Two DataHub actions for a workflow engine:

- `CreateDataProductAction` creates a data product when a request is accepted, and can set its lifecycle stage in the same step.
- `SetDataProductLifecycleAction` sets the lifecycle stage on a data product that already exists.

The engine installs this package from **Extra Pip Libraries**, then loads the step `type` as a Python class.

## Build the wheel

From this directory:

```bash
python3 -m pip wheel . --no-deps -w dist
```

That writes `dist/data_product_actions-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl`. A wheel is a zip. The pip name is `data-product-actions` on purpose. The engine already installs `https://github.com/brock-acryl/datahub-workflow-actions/archive/main.zip` under the name `datahub-workflow-actions`. uv rejects two URLs for the same package name, which is what happened with the earlier wheel.

On the engine ingestion source, set **Extra Pip Libraries** to both, and keep the engine zip:

```json
[
  "https://github.com/brock-acryl/datahub-workflow-actions/archive/main.zip",
  "https://github.com/steve751/steves-workflow-actions/raw/refs/heads/main/data-product-actions/dist/data_product_actions-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl"
]
```

Replace the old `datahub_workflow_actions-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl` URL. Save the source and run it once so the executor installs the wheel before the rules fire. The step `type` strings do not change. They still import `datahub_workflow_actions`.

## What the workflow form must collect

| Field id | Required | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | yes, for create | Display name of the new data product |
| `domain` | yes, for create | URN of a domain that already exists, such as `urn:li:domain:marketing` |
| `description` | no | Description stored on the new data product |

`{{ fields.name }}` reads the first value of the form field whose id is `name`. `{{ entity.urn }}` is the entity the workflow was launched from.

`lifecycle_stage` must be a lifecycle stage URN that exists in your instance and applies to data products. `urn:li:lifecycleStageType:DRAFT` and `urn:li:lifecycleStageType:PUBLISHED` in the examples are placeholders. Replace them with stages from your catalog. The caller needs permission to manage data products on the target domain.

## Example: create a product when the request is approved

Use this when someone submits a form and an approver accepts it. The create step also sets the lifecycle stage on the product it just created, so you do not need the new URN in a later step.

`rules.json` in this folder is the same document, with both rules.

```json
{
  "id": "create-data-product",
  "name": "Create data product when approved",
  "enabled": true,
  "on": {
    "operation": "COMPLETED",
    "result": "ACCEPTED"
  },
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "create-data-product",
      "type": "datahub_workflow_actions.create_product:CreateDataProductAction",
      "params": {
        "name": "{{ fields.name }}",
        "domain": "{{ fields.domain }}",
        "description": "{{ fields.description }}",
        "lifecycle_stage": "urn:li:lifecycleStageType:DRAFT",
        "workflow_urn": "urn:li:actionWorkflow:8a96234f-96b7-465b-a633-579d8bf2cf8d"
      }
    }
  ],
  "engineId": "urn:li:dataHubIngestionSource:0ae5de7b-d931-4e18-a87c-44bd3951b368",
  "workflowUrn": "urn:li:actionWorkflow:8a96234f-96b7-465b-a633-579d8bf2cf8d",
  "description": "When the request is approved, create the data product in the chosen domain and set its lifecycle stage."
}
```

A submitted form like this:

```json
{
  "name": ["Customer 360"],
  "domain": ["urn:li:domain:marketing"],
  "description": ["Customer data product"]
}
```

creates a data product named Customer 360 in `urn:li:domain:marketing`, then sets its lifecycle stage to the URN in `lifecycle_stage`.

Optional params on the create step:

- `id` — fixed id used in the data product URN. Omit it and DataHub generates one.
- `parent_data_product` — URN of an existing parent data product.
- `workflow_urn` — ignore completion events from any other workflow.

## Example: change the lifecycle stage of an existing data product

Launch this workflow from the data product page. `{{ entity.urn }}` is that product.

```json
{
  "id": "set-data-product-lifecycle",
  "name": "Set data product lifecycle stage",
  "enabled": true,
  "on": {
    "operation": "COMPLETED",
    "result": "ACCEPTED"
  },
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "set-lifecycle-stage",
      "type": "datahub_workflow_actions.set_lifecycle:SetDataProductLifecycleAction",
      "params": {
        "entity": "{{ entity.urn }}",
        "lifecycle_stage": "urn:li:lifecycleStageType:PUBLISHED",
        "workflow_urn": "urn:li:actionWorkflow:8a96234f-96b7-465b-a633-579d8bf2cf8d"
      }
    }
  ],
  "engineId": "urn:li:dataHubIngestionSource:0ae5de7b-d931-4e18-a87c-44bd3951b368",
  "workflowUrn": "urn:li:actionWorkflow:8a96234f-96b7-465b-a633-579d8bf2cf8d",
  "description": "When the request is approved, set the lifecycle stage on the data product the workflow was launched from."
}
```

To take the product URN from the form instead of the page it was launched from, set `entity` to `{{ fields.data_product }}` and add a form field whose id is `data_product`.

## Running it as a DataHub Action pipeline

The same classes are registered as `create_data_product` and `set_data_product_lifecycle` on the `datahub_actions.action.plugins` entry point. A pipeline config that listens for the accepted workflow is in `examples/actions-pipeline.json`. Run it with the actions CLI after the wheel is installed in that environment:

```bash
datahub actions -c examples/actions-pipeline.json
```

The engine rule above is the path that uses Extra Pip Libraries. The pipeline file is the path that uses `datahub actions` directly.
